  "editorReview": {
    "text": "The Scottish tidal station offers a masterclass in atmospheric pressure, with Iona’s methodical cataloging—circling tide tables, counting paces—creating a distinctive investigative rhythm that feels genuinely lived-in. The clues are fair and cumulative, earning their revelations honestly through precise sensory details, like the intake channel walls rising \"like the sides of a tomb.\" However, the prose occasionally suffers from mechanical repetition, and the urge to over-explain—explicitly stating that a murder was disguised as an accident—undermines the reader’s own deductive pleasure. While",
